    <node id="Narrator">
      <data key="d0">Narrator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Narrator is the unnamed first-person diarist of the diary passage and the central figure through whom all events, actions, and observations are recounted. The Narrator is male: he is the husband of Narrator’s Wife and the son of Narrator’s Father and Mother. He is entrusted by My Lord with overseeing affairs during My Lord’s absence and receives orders from the Lord Chancellor and Mr. Edward Montagu, working at the Office. He records daily activities including receiving instructions, hosting dinner guests, dining with My Lady, and managing money, goods, and household matters.

The Narrator’s travels and business are extensively documented. He journeys along the Thames to Greenwich, Woolwich, and Whitehall, and makes overland trips by horseback to Ware, Barkway, Brampton, Cambridge, and Impington. He visits his father, deals with an uncle’s corpse and will, and works on uncle’s estate affairs, consulting Roger Pepys at the Assizes about the will. He is troubled by estate matters, walks around Portholme, and attends to correspondence with his father.

Socially and professionally, the Narrator dines with Lord Crew and My Lady, waits on the Duke of York, visits relatives, sick acquaintances, and family, attends church, and gives fruit and wine to visitors. He reads The Spanish Gypsey, hires the Margate Hoy, records boating trips and social meals, goes to the Opera, and visits a baboon. He also argues with Pall, settles Maid Jane’s service, handles family finances, discusses a proposed wife for Tom, expresses worry about Tom, and notes dissatisfaction with the Court and Clergy while intending to subscribe to the Benevolence. Throughout, the Narrator remains unnamed, yet his first-person perspective gives the diary its continuity and personal detail.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-049</data>
