    <node id="Sir W. Pen">
      <data key="d0">Sir W. Pen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir W. Pen was a knight and colleague of Samuel Pepys, frequently appearing in Pepys’s diary as a companion in both professional and social settings. He traveled by water with Pepys and Mr. Gauden to Woolwich and Deptford, rode with Pepys to Moorefields and later accompanied him to Deptford, and also came home with Pepys and his wife, drank with them, and had a daughter who had recently returned from Ireland. He was a guest at Sir W. Batten’s Sunday dinner.

In his official capacity, Sir W. Pen waited with Pepys on the Duke of York, and the two jointly reported Navy finances to the Duke. He transported Pepys and Captain Ferrers in his coach, and he socialized with Pepys at the Dolphin and at the Theatre. On one occasion he was at the Theatre with Lady Batten and later at the Dolphin, where Pepys followed him.

Sir W. Pen’s house was also a regular gathering place for Pepys and others, where they sat and drank until late, with one visit made at night. Pepys visited him on the 23rd after putting on mourning, and another account likewise describes the diarist visiting him while in mourning. Finally, Sir W. Pen was the recipient of a counterfeit letter written by Pepys to mock him about a stolen tankard.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046</data>
