    <node id="My Lord">
      <data key="d0">My Lord</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">My Lord is the name Samuel Pepys uses for his noble patron, evidently Edward Montagu, the man appointed ambassador to bring over Katherine of Braganza and later chosen Master of Trinity House. He was a nobleman connected to households at Whitehall, the Wardrobe, and My Lady’s household, and Pepys acted as his servant and deputy, at one point being sworn as his deputy at the Privy Seal. My Lord was preparing for a sea voyage, for which Pepys obtained the signing of his imprest bill of £1,000. He was often at Whitehall conducting business, and gave Pepys instructions regarding the Wardrobe, especially concerning Mr. Townsend. He required £300 worth of cloth to be given as presents in Barbary, and cloth purchased by Pepys’s father was intended for him.

Before departing, My Lord took leave of the King, was seen off by Sir Robert Slingsby, and sailed aboard the Dutch Yacht, firing a five-gun salute to Pepys. He later stayed at the Wardrobe to take leave of friends and ordered household items to be sent after him. His mission included going to Algiers to settle business and put the fleet in order, then returning to Lisbon with three ships. During his absence, Pepys oversaw preparations and wrote letters to him, at times having no news of his whereabouts when reaching London. On one occasion My Lord was recovering from a fever and wind in his right side but was reported ready to go aboard within a day or two.

Pepys handled numerous matters for My Lord: he put his papers in order, wrote to him about completed business with My Lady’s household, and had letters ready for him, though some were not ready when Captain Isham called. My Lord also advised Pepys about a land purchase, sent him messages, and received a letter written late at night. He was not universally praised, however: when he was expected at the Privy Seal but failed to appear all afternoon, he was criticized for delaying business and treating clerks harshly. Overall, My Lord is depicted as Pepys’s influential and often absent patron, involved in naval, diplomatic, and household affairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Earldom of Kent">
      <data key="d0">Earldom of Kent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The noble title that, according to the anecdote told by Lord Crew, falls to the young Lord of Kent while he is serving Lord Bedford in Queen Elizabeth's time.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-002</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Diarist">
      <data key="d0">The Diarist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The unnamed writer of the diary entry who records personal activities such as giving money to the wife, dining at the Lord's lodgings, doing business at the office, playing bowls, drinking and singing with Sir William Pen, traveling to Greenwich with Captain Lambert, and visiting Mr. Holland's house in Bridewell.&lt;SEP&gt;First-person narrator of the diary entry. The Diarist is the husband of My Wife, receives advice from My Lord about money, and records visits and conversations.&lt;SEP&gt;The unnamed narrator of the diary, who attends to business, dines with acquaintances, practices music, sees a play, and plans to buy land.&lt;SEP&gt;First-person speaker of the diary passage; a London man who conducts office business, visits Westminster Hall, takes singing lessons, attends operas and plays, and has family members.&lt;SEP&gt;The person who writes the diary, referred to as "I"; he makes visits, attends the theatre, and deals with family and household matters.&lt;SEP&gt;The first-person narrator of the diary entry who waits on the Duke of York, dines with My Lady, attends the Theatre and the Opera, and receives letters from Mr. Creed.&lt;SEP&gt;The first-person narrator who accompanies the ladies, visits Christ's Hospitall, talks with My Lady, drinks at the Mitre, and reports on family and financial matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Diarist's Wife">
      <data key="d0">The Diarist's Wife</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The wife of the diary writer, who receives £4 from the diarist to spend on lace and other items for herself.&lt;SEP&gt;The wife of the diarist; she tells him about the robbery and accompanies him to Whitehall.&lt;SEP&gt;The wife of the Diarist, reported to be in good health.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Lord">
      <data key="d0">The Lord</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nobleman at whose lodgings in Westminster the diarist dines with Ned Dickering.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ned Dickering">
      <data key="d0">Ned Dickering</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man who dines with the diarist and the Lord at the Lord's lodgings in Westminster.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir William Pen">
      <data key="d0">Sir William Pen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A companion of the diarist who joins in playing bowls at Sir R. Slingsby's alley, walks home with the diarist, and spends the evening on the garden leads talking, singing, and drinking claret.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the two "Sir Williams" who traveled by coach to Walthamstow; appears with Sir W. Batten in the party.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-029</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir R. Slingsby">
      <data key="d0">Sir R. Slingsby</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man in whose alley the diarist and Sir William Pen play bowls and afterward drink and talk.&lt;SEP&gt;A man The Narrator visited after supper; with his brother and The Narrator, he went to My Lord's residence at The Wardrobe.&lt;SEP&gt;Person at whose lodgings the Narrator sleeps while the Narrator's house is being painted and whitened.&lt;SEP&gt;A knight who, with the Narrator, went to inspect Sir R. Ford's house before sitting down at the office.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Captain Lambert">
      <data key="d0">Captain Lambert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An officer who has just been made captain of the ship Norwich, accompanies the diarist by water to Greenwich, and later goes with the diarist to Bridewell.&lt;SEP&gt;A host and acquaintance who entertained the diarist and his wife at his house.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-011</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
