    <node id="Captain Lambert's House">
      <data key="d0">Captain Lambert's House</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The house of Captain Lambert on the water route back from Greenwich; it is described as very handsome and neat, with a fine prospect at the top.&lt;SEP&gt;The house belonging to Captain Lambert and Captain Lambert's Wife, visited by the diarist and the diarist's wife, containing a turret-garden and a harpsicon, and where a little witty child was kept.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-011</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Garden Leads">
      <data key="d0">The Garden Leads</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lead-covered area adjoining the garden where the diarist plays the flageolette and where the diarist and Sir William Pen stay talking and singing in the evening.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-003</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Captain">
      <data key="d0">Captain</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Captain is a man who accompanied the narrator to Bridewell at the start of the passage.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
