    <node id="Sir William Batten">
      <data key="d0">Sir William Batten</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir William Batten is a man who came from Chatham with My Lady Batten and whose house the narrator visited.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="My Lady Batten">
      <data key="d0">My Lady Batten</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">My Lady Batten is the woman who came from Chatham with Sir William Batten and was much troubled with toothache.&lt;SEP&gt;Wife of Sir W. Batten; attended the burial of Sir John Lawson's daughter and received funeral rings.&lt;SEP&gt;The wife of Sir W. Batten, present at the Theatre with Sir W. Pen.&lt;SEP&gt;A woman who, with her daughter, looks askew at the Narrator’s wife because the wife does not seek their acquaintance.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Shepley">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Shepley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Shepley is a man who dined with the narrator at the Cook's.&lt;SEP&gt;Companion who goes out with the Narrator to drink Lambeth ale at a nearby house.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Rawlinson">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Rawlinson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Rawlinson is a man from whom the narrator sought advice about answering Uncle Robert's letter.&lt;SEP&gt;Host at whose house My Father, the Narrator, and relatives meet and read The Will.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle Robert">
      <data key="d0">Uncle Robert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncle Robert is the narrator's uncle who sent the narrator a letter offering a purchase of land adjacent to Uncle Robert's own lands.&lt;SEP&gt;The diarist's uncle, who had a dizzy spell and whose illness prompted My Father to visit him and look after his business.&lt;SEP&gt;Brother of Samuel Pepys's father, living at Brampton, whose condition was uncertain when his brother came to visit him.&lt;SEP&gt;An uncle of the narrator; mentioned in relation to the narrator's father's financial situation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445843</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Narrator's Wife">
      <data key="d0">Narrator's Wife</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Narrator's Wife is the wife of the first-person narrator, frequently appearing alongside him in domestic, social, and leisure contexts. She walks with him on the leads before they go to bed, receives money from him for lace and other items, and gives linen to the Narrator. Her health is sometimes delicate: on one occasion she is found not sick but out of order, and one of her petticoats was later made into a faced white coat.

She actively shares in the Narrator's social life. She dines at home with him, attends church with him, and joins him in outings, including going by coach to dinner at Thos Pepys's and then to the theatre. She also dines at the Wardrobe, goes to Drury Lane, meets a son of Lord Somersett, and enjoys seeing the royal party at the Theatre. She hosts visitors, visits Sir W. Pen's daughter, and is criticized by Lady Batten and her daughter. She also requests the Narrator to help Balty, showing her involvement in family and patronage matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-049</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chatham">
      <data key="d0">Chatham</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chatham is the place from which Sir William Batten and My Lady Batten came.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cook's">
      <data key="d0">Cook's</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cook's is an eating-house where the narrator dined with Mr. Shepley and Mr. Creed.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Theatre">
      <data key="d0">Theatre</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Theatre is the playhouse where the narrator saw Bartholomew Faire performed.&lt;SEP&gt;The place to which the narrator and wife went after dinner to see a play.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-045</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Shop">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Shop</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tobacco Shop under Temple Bar gate is where the narrator and Mr. Creed went up and drank Lambeth ale.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bartholomew Faire">
      <data key="d0">Bartholomew Faire</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bartholomew Faire is a play acted at the Theatre, described by the narrator as admirable but too profane and abusive.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle Robert's Letter">
      <data key="d0">Uncle Robert's Letter</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncle Robert's Letter is a letter containing an offer to the narrator to purchase land adjoining Uncle Robert's own lands.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Holland's Mother">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Holland's Mother</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Holland's mother was present at Mr. Holland's house in Bridewell while the narrator visited.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Two Young Gentlewomen">
      <data key="d0">Two Young Gentlewomen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Two unnamed young gentlewomen came to see Mr. Holland; one of them could play the violin fairly well.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temple Bar Gate">
      <data key="d0">Temple Bar Gate</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A London landmark gate under which the tobacco shop visited by the narrator and Mr. Creed was located.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="My Wife">
      <data key="d0">My Wife</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wife of The Diarist. My Wife wears a black silk gown laced with black gimp lace and accompanies The Diarist to My Lady's residence at The Wardrobe.&lt;SEP&gt;The Narrator's wife, whom the Narrator met at The Wardrobe.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Narrator's Father">
      <data key="d0">Narrator's Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Narrator's Father is the father of the narrator and of Tom, and is described as the owner of a trade that has now passed to Tom. He is an active presence in family and social life, dining at the narrator's house, being visited by the narrator for supper on another occasion, and going with the narrator to the Bear Tavern. The narrator also writes to him when no word has arrived, and he is said to be staying alone at Brampton in good health. While at Brampton, he reads the uncle's will in the garden and, with the narrator, looks over the uncle's papers and clothes. He walks with his son through Holborn and plans to go to Brampton, and he is the intended recipient of money that the narrator wishes to lay out.

His involvement extends to business and household matters. He participates in deciding to buy scarlet, purple, black cloth, and linen. He is also involved in arrangements and the dispute concerning Pall, in settling accounts, and in visits to relatives. On one occasion he is present at an alehouse with Uncle Fenner, after which he dismisses the servant Ned.

The father also plays a role in discussions about Tom's marriage. He dines at the narrator's house and later meets Mr. Evans at an alehouse to discuss the proposed marriage for Tom, which is also described as a match between Tom and the broker's daughter. These discussions are part of his broader engagement with the family’s affairs and Tom’s future.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-049</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Pierce">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Pierce</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Acquaintance of The Diarist near Whitehall. Mr. Pierce joins The Diarist and Mr. Symons at the Swan Tavern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Symons">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Symons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dancing master. Mr. Symons goes to sea with My Lord and goes with Mr. Pierce and The Diarist to the Swan Tavern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dean Fuller">
      <data key="d0">Dean Fuller</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dean who meets The Diarist at Whitehall. Dean Fuller discusses the Bishop of Galloway's practice of admitting people into orders.&lt;SEP&gt;A dean whom Pepys took along when going to dine with My Lady at the Wardrobe.&lt;SEP&gt;A dean whom The Diarist took along when going to dine with My Lady.&lt;SEP&gt;A visitor who comes to the diary narrator's house to see the narrator just after the narrator's return and accompanies the narrator to My Lady's house.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-032</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bishop of Galloway">
      <data key="d0">Bishop of Galloway</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bishop criticized by Dean Fuller for taking liberty in admitting anyone into orders, including Roundtree.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roundtree">
      <data key="d0">Roundtree</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Simple mechanic formerly in the fleet. Roundtree is admitted into orders by the Bishop of Galloway.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="W. Howe">
      <data key="d0">W. Howe</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Companion who joins The Diarist at Whitehall, goes to The Wardrobe, and sings at Jane Shore's Tower.&lt;SEP&gt;Person who was to bring My Lord's things; his late arrival made My Lord angry, and The Narrator later takes leave of him.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-008</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Charles II">
      <data key="d0">Charles II</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King of England. Charles II makes My Lord an ambassador and is married to Katherine of Braganza.&lt;SEP&gt;King who married Katherine of Braganza on May 21, 1662; after the death of Charles II, Katherine lived at Somerset House and later returned to Portugal.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Katherine of Braganza">
      <data key="d0">Katherine of Braganza</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Queen brought over by My Lord. Katherine of Braganza is the daughter of John IV of Portugal and the wife of Charles II.&lt;SEP&gt;Daughter of John IV of Portugal, born 1638; married Charles II on May 21, 1662; after the death of Charles II lived at Somerset House; returned to Portugal and became Regent of Portugal in 1704; died December 31, 1705.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="John IV of Portugal">
      <data key="d0">John IV of Portugal</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King of Portugal and father of Katherine of Braganza.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
