    <node id="Uncle Wight">
      <data key="d0">Uncle Wight</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncle Wight is a relative—specifically an uncle—of the diarist Samuel Pepys, appearing in Pepys’s diary as a recurring family figure. In one account, he was out of town, which led Pepys to seek advice from Mr. Rawlinson instead; in another, he was not at home when Pepys called at his house.

Uncle Wight was involved in family business and social visits. Pepys visited him at the Exchange to discuss a purchase at Brampton, and on another occasion encountered him at the Exchange, where Uncle Wight was discontented with Pepys’s father. He also met Pepys at the Exchequer and brought him home for dinner. He went with Pepys to the Mitre, where he was upset that Pepys’s father had not told him about the Brampton trip, and where he was informed of Robert Pepys’s death. Later, Pepys and his father found Uncle Wight at a Coffee House, and he agreed to the meeting for the reading of a will.

In further family gatherings, Uncle Wight went out with Pepys and Pepys’s father to an alehouse because the house was too hot, and he also received a visit and supper from them. Overall, Uncle Wight appears as an uncle whose interactions with Pepys were marked by family news, business discussions concerning Brampton, and shared meals or meetings at various London locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
