    <node id="Robert Pepys">
      <data key="d0">Robert Pepys</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Samuel Pepys's uncle from Brampton; Robert Pepys has daily fits of stupefaction, dies, and is laid in a coffin in the hall at Brampton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Batersby">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Batersby</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An apothecary at the Mitre who advises that leeches and bleeding could cure Robert Pepys if Robert Pepys's stopped piles were opened.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Opera">
      <data key="d0">The Opera</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Opera or opera season that has recently begun and is thought by Samuel Pepys to be drawing audiences away from the Theatre.&lt;SEP&gt;A theatre that begins again with The Witts, performed for the first time with scenes, attended by the King, Duke, and Duchess.&lt;SEP&gt;Venue where Captain Ferrers and the narrator saw The Witts and where Queen of Bohemia was present.&lt;SEP&gt;The place to which the narrator takes the narrator's wife to see the play "The Witts."&lt;SEP&gt;The theatre where the Narrator sees “Hamlet, Prince of Denmark” performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Mitre">
      <data key="d0">The Mitre</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A tavern where Samuel Pepys and Uncle Wight go and are merry and where Mr. Batersby is present.&lt;SEP&gt;A tavern where the Narrator takes Capt. Isham and gives him a glass of sack.&lt;SEP&gt;A tavern on Fenchurch Street where the Diarist and Mr. Moore drink wine before parting.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St. Martin's">
      <data key="d0">St. Martin's</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A place in London where Samuel Pepys buys a pair of boots before beginning the ride to Brampton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Post House">
      <data key="d0">Post House</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A post house from which Samuel Pepys and the messenger set out on horseback toward Brampton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Emerods">
      <data key="d0">Emerods</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical condition, also called piles, that Samuel Pepys thinks Robert Pepys may have and on which Mr. Batersby bases one possible cure.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fits of Stupefaction">
      <data key="d0">Fits of Stupefaction</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurring episodes of stupor that, according to what Samuel Pepys hears, Robert Pepys experiences for ten to twelve hours each day.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Messenger">
      <data key="d0">The Messenger</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An unnamed messenger who wakes Samuel Pepys with the news of Robert Pepys's death and later rides with Samuel Pepys to Brampton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Samuel Pepys's Father's House">
      <data key="d0">Samuel Pepys's Father's House</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The residence of Samuel Pepys's father, where Samuel Pepys calls on the 4th and hears about Robert Pepys's fits of stupefaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Narrator's Uncle">
      <data key="d0">Narrator's Uncle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Deceased uncle of the narrator; the uncle's corpse was kept in a coffin at Brampton and later moved to the yard, and the uncle's will, estate, and burial were central to the events.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tom Trice">
      <data key="d0">Tom Trice</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Man who filed a caveat against the narrator and the narrator's father on behalf of Tom Trice's mother.&lt;SEP&gt;Tom Trice is one of the two Trices in the will dispute; his arrival is awaited before an issue can be reached, and he later takes part in the evening argument and agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;A man with whom the diary narrator talks at Gorum's House.&lt;SEP&gt;A man involved in proving an uncle's will; Tom Trice signs the copy, receives the original to prove, and takes the narrator and the narrator's father to a judge.&lt;SEP&gt;A man who arranges the probate of a will for the narrator.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tom Trice's Mother">
      <data key="d0">Tom Trice's Mother</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mother of Tom Trice; the narrator's uncle gave her nothing, and Tom Trice acted on her behalf in the legal caveat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr Taylor">
      <data key="d0">Mr Taylor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Person who buried the narrator's uncle at the church in Brampton.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr Turners">
      <data key="d0">Mr Turners</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Person who preached the funeral sermon at the burial of the narrator's uncle and spoke of the uncle's honesty as well known.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle's Will">
      <data key="d0">Uncle's Will</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Legal will left by the narrator's uncle; read in the garden by the narrator and the narrator's father; gave the narrator little until the narrator's father's death and gave nothing to Tom Trice's mother.&lt;SEP&gt;The will of an uncle of the diarist; the diarist discusses it with relatives, and the uncle's estate is about L500.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncle's Will is the last will and testament of the Narrator's uncle; reading the will with Mr. Moore suggested more trouble to the Narrator.&lt;SEP&gt;The last will of an uncle of the narrator; it is examined before Dr. Williams and Tom Trice, a copy is signed, and the original is given to Tom Trice to prove.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle's Papers">
      <data key="d0">Uncle's Papers</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Written records left by the narrator's uncle; examined by the narrator and the narrator's father, but found in confusion and hard to understand.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle's Estate">
      <data key="d0">Uncle's Estate</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Wealth and property left by the narrator's uncle; the estate appeared to be much less valuable than expected and was a major source of trouble.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Coffin">
      <data key="d0">Coffin</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The coffin containing the corpse of the Narrator's Uncle; it first stood on joint-stools in the hall at Brampton, then was moved to the yard overnight because the corpse began to smell.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncle's Burial">
      <data key="d0">Uncle's Burial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The burial ceremony for the Narrator's Uncle, held in the afternoon on the Lord's day at the church in Brampton, conducted by Mr Taylor, with a funeral sermon preached by Mr Turners.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tom Trice's Caveat">
      <data key="d0">Tom Trice's Caveat</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal caveat filed by Tom Trice on behalf of Tom Trice's Mother, opposing the Narrator and the Narrator's Father in the matter of the Narrator's Uncle's estate.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Father">
      <data key="d0">Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The father of the narrator. Father is at Brampton, walks around Portholme with the narrator, and is shielded from worry over estate troubles.&lt;SEP&gt;The father of The Narrator, who owns a house in London and weighs whether to profit from it or leave it to Tom.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator's father, who goes with the narrator to Dr. Williams and Tom Trice for legal business and is sworn before a judge.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Philips">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Philips</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man who returns home from London in the latter part of the week and is consulted for counsel about the estate.&lt;SEP&gt;A man involved in the argument with the narrator; later one of the witnesses to William Luffe's acknowledgment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Robert Barnwell">
      <data key="d0">Robert Barnwell</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man who dines with the narrator and Father at Brampton on a Sunday.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Portholme">
      <data key="d0">Portholme</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A field area near Brampton that the narrator and Father walk around and view; it is described as very pleasant.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hinchingbroke">
      <data key="d0">Hinchingbroke</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A place near Brampton visited by the narrator and Father; it is currently dirty from building work by Lord, which is expected to make it magnificent.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lord">
      <data key="d0">Lord</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nobleman whose building work at Hinchingbroke is making the place magnificent.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cambridge">
      <data key="d0">Cambridge</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Town in which Talbot Pepys was M.P. and Recorder, and the seat of Trinity Hall.&lt;SEP&gt;A city to which the narrator rides early in the morning; the narrator visits Christ College and King's College Chapel there and departs at noon.&lt;SEP&gt;A town where the Assizes begin, and where The Narrator plans to go to meet Roger Pepys.&lt;SEP&gt;Town toward which Samuel Pepys set out on horseback after preparing to leave.&lt;SEP&gt;The city the narrator reaches; it contains Castle Hill, Trinity Hall, the Rose, and the University.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-030</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Christ College">
      <data key="d0">Christ College</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cambridge college visited by the narrator, where Brother John is found in bed.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brother John">
      <data key="d0">Brother John</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Brother of the narrator, present at Christ College in Cambridge and found in bed at eight o'clock.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="King's College Chapel">
      <data key="d0">King's College Chapel</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A chapel in Cambridge visited by the narrator; scholars in surplices are at service there, and Dr. Fairbrother is met there.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. Fairbrother">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Fairbrother</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man met at King's College Chapel who goes with the narrator to the Rose Tavern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rose Tavern">
      <data key="d0">Rose Tavern</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A tavern in Cambridge where the narrator, Dr. Fairbrother, Mr. Turner, and Mr. Sanchy gather and are merry.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Turner's Wife">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Turner's Wife</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wife of Mr. Turner; she is sent for and joins the company at the Rose Tavern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Sanchy">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Sanchy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man of Magdalen who joins the narrator and others at the Rose Tavern.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magdalen">
      <data key="d0">Magdalen</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The institution or college named in the opening phrase as the affiliation of one of the gentlemen with whom the Narrator was merry before riding toward Impington.&lt;SEP&gt;An institution, probably a college, with which Mr. Sanchy is associated.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cousin Angier">
      <data key="d0">Cousin Angier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A cousin of the narrator in Cambridge from whom the narrator takes leave when departing.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Estate">
      <data key="d0">Estate</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The property and financial affairs, called the estate, that the narrator and the family find less valuable than expected and are trying to settle.&lt;SEP&gt;An inheritance left to the Diarist, reported by the Diarist as L200 a year in land besides other moneys, and publicly claimed to raise the Diarist's own esteem.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Estate Papers">
      <data key="d0">Estate Papers</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Documents belonging to the estate; the papers are unsorted and in confusion and vex the narrator.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Copyhold Land">
      <data key="d0">Copyhold Land</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Land held by copyhold tenure that is part of the estate; without the proper surrenders, the land would pass to the heir at law.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heir at Law">
      <data key="d0">Heir at Law</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The legal heir who would receive the copyhold land if the required surrenders were not made.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Copyhold Surrenders">
      <data key="d0">Copyhold Surrenders</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Legal documents surrendering the copyhold land; the family missed the surrenders, so the land would not pass to them but to the heir at law.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Talbot Pepys">
      <data key="d0">Talbot Pepys</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrator's old uncle, found sitting all alone at Impington, where he could hardly see but was otherwise lively. According to the editorial note, Talbot Pepys was the sixth son of John Pepys of Impington, born 1583, educated at Trinity Hall, Cambridge, called to the bar at the Middle Temple in 1605, served as M.P. for Cambridge in 1625 and Recorder of Cambridge from 1624 to 1660, and died of the plague in March 1666.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="John Pepys of Impington">
      <data key="d0">John Pepys of Impington</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Father of Talbot Pepys, according to the editorial note, which identifies Talbot as the sixth son of John Pepys of Impington.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. John Pepys">
      <data key="d0">Dr. John Pepys</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A relative who joined the narrator and Talbot Pepys at Impington; the narrator read over the will with him, and he gave advice on its sufficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Angier">
      <data key="d0">Angier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrator's cousin, from whom the narrator took leave before riding from Magdalen toward Impington.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stankes">
      <data key="d0">Stankes</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A man with whom the narrator and his family agreed to have care of their business in their absence; they considered themselves fortunate to find him as their bailiff.&lt;SEP&gt;Stankes is a person who, with his wife, dines with the narrator's household.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Serjeant Bernard">
      <data key="d0">Serjeant Bernard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person mentioned as having come home into the country on the evening of the 19th, according to what the narrator hears.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jane Perkin">
      <data key="d0">Jane Perkin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person named in the narrator's description of his aunt's base, hypocritical tricks; the phrase "of whom we make great" is left incomplete.&lt;SEP&gt;Jane Perkin is a person of whom the narrator's household makes great use; she tells the narrator's household about the aunt's hypocritical tricks.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Impington">
      <data key="d0">Impington</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Place where the narrator found his old uncle Talbot Pepys sitting alone and where the will was read over; also the narrator's destination after leaving Magdalen.&lt;SEP&gt;A village to which the narrator rides with Roger Pepys and his brothers and where the narrator sleeps in the best chamber.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-030</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gravely">
      <data key="d0">Gravely</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Place the narrator rode to with much effort to inquire about a surrender of his uncle's estate in the hands of copyholders.&lt;SEP&gt;Place whose affairs Dr. John Williams knew well.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Offord">
      <data key="d0">Offord</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A place the narrator rode to in the course of going up and down all the family lands while overseeing business.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sturtlow">
      <data key="d0">Sturtlow</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A place the narrator rode to in the course of going up and down all the family lands while overseeing business.&lt;SEP&gt;The property named in the surrender made by the narrator's uncle.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trinity Hall, Cambridge">
      <data key="d0">Trinity Hall, Cambridge</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">College at Cambridge where Talbot Pepys was educated.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Middle Temple">
      <data key="d0">Middle Temple</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Inn of court at which Talbot Pepys was called to the bar in 1605.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Will">
      <data key="d0">The Will</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal will read over by the narrator at Impington with Dr. John Pepys and Talbot Pepys; three witnesses to the will were all legatees, and Mr. Moore advised this would not invalidate it.&lt;SEP&gt;The Will is a legal document owned and shown by the narrator during the dispute, to be resolved in discussions with the Trice family and Sir Robert Bernard.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal document read at Mr. Rawlinson's during the family meeting.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal document whose probate is performed by Tom Trice and gives the narrator and the narrator's father satisfaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Narrator's Father">
      <data key="d0">The Narrator's Father</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrator's father goes to Goody Gorum's with Tom Trice and is involved in the business of settling the estate.&lt;SEP&gt;The parent of the Narrator, present at Brampton; the Narrator tried to seem cheerful to the Father, and the two walked about the fields talking and spent several days arranging family affairs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Narrator's father, who lives at Brampton, is found very well by The Narrator, discerns that The Narrator has been drinking, and later goes with The Narrator to Goody Gorum's.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator's father, who returned from Brampton that day and came to the narrator's house.&lt;SEP&gt;Father of the narrator; dines at the narrator's home and plans to go into the country.&lt;SEP&gt;The father of the narrator; meets the narrator by appointment and visits Dr. Williams with the narrator.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-041</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Narrator's Aunt">
      <data key="d0">The Narrator's Aunt</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrator's aunt continues in base, hypocritical tricks; she is party to the will dispute and agrees to quit the house in exchange for L10.&lt;SEP&gt;The aunt of the Narrator, described as continuing in base, hypocritical tricks; the passage breaks off mid-sentence.&lt;SEP&gt;The narrator's aunt, who was to receive L10 and to quit the house under the agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;The Narrator's aunt, who has left the house in Brampton; The Narrator is glad of the aunt's departure though it costs the family L10.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-031</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir Robert Bernard">
      <data key="d0">Sir Robert Bernard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir Robert Bernard, also called Serjeant Bernard, is a knight living in Huntingdon who mediates discussions about a Will and hosts a dinner at his home.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jaspar Trice">
      <data key="d0">Jaspar Trice</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jaspar Trice is one of the two Trices involved in the will dispute; he converses with the narrator at Sir Robert Bernard's, later drinks at Mother's, and argues in the evening.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Greene">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Greene</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Greene joins meetings at Goody Gorum's and the evening argument about the will.&lt;SEP&gt;A man involved in an argument with the narrator that resulted in an agreement.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445866</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Phillips">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Phillips</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Phillips, also written Philips, is an acquaintance in Huntingdon with whom the narrator spends time and drinks ale, and who participates in the evening argument.&lt;SEP&gt;A man whom The Narrator tries to meet early and later drinks with at Huntingdon; the text spells the name as both Phillips and Philips.</data>
