    <node id="Tom">
      <data key="d0">Tom</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tom, also called Thomas and identified as Pepys’s brother, is the figure described throughout. He came to Pepys during the day, went with the narrator to the Bear Tavern and was given wine, and dined with the narrator while talking privately about the family trade and the London house. Tom was left in charge of maintaining his father’s trade, and the narrator feared that he would fail or “miscarry” for lack of brains and care. Tom also helped settle accounts.

In connection with marriage, Tom was the proposed husband for a broker’s daughter, described in other accounts as Mr. Evans’s daughter. The narrator and his father considered taking her as Tom’s wife, but the narrator feared the match would not take place. Overall, these descriptions present Tom as a brother engaged in the family business and as the subject of a doubtful marriage arrangement.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-049</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445871</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="London">
      <data key="d0">London</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">London is a central locale in the narrated account, serving as both a point of origin and a destination for travel. The narrator and his companions travel toward London by boat, and on one occasion the narrator rides on horseback to the city, where he finds his own household, his father's household, and My Lady all well. London is also the city from which the diarist set out on a journey to the house where he was staying, and from which Mr. Philips returns home toward the latter end of the week, after which the family seeks his counsel.

Within London, several notable events and associations occur. Mr. Moore is based in London when he sends advice to the narrator, and Father owns a house in the city. London is also the source of a hot venison pasty that was brought to Walthamstow. Additionally, the city is connected to religious observance: a form of prayer titled *A Form of Prayer* was to be used in London on the 12th of June, one of the special days appointed for the General Fast. The narrator traveled to London on the 22nd, reinforcing its role as a recurring destination in the diary narrative.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-032</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445871</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Narrator">
      <data key="d0">The Narrator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Narrator is the unnamed first-person diarist and central figure of the diary account, and a member of the Pepys family. In addition to recording office business and daily life, he acts as deputy to My Lord and moves through London locations including the Wardrobe, Whitehall, Westminster Hall, the Privy Seal, Fleet Street, a tavern near the Savoy, Deptford, the theatre, Clerkenwell, and Bartholomew Fair. A specific diary entry for 11 and 12 June describes him going about business, writing to the Duke, meeting My Lord, and visiting several places.

The Narrator is also closely involved in family and estate matters. He is part of a family with his father, mother, uncle, and brother Tom, and he travels among Huntingdon, Brampton, Impington, Gravely, Graveley, Yelling, Offord, and Sturtlow. He reads over an uncle’s will, is engaged in a will dispute, negotiates with relatives and legal contacts, and arranges for the management of family lands. He treats friends at Magdalen, takes leave of cousin Angier, visits relatives, dines with Mr. Moore, and discusses public affairs. His left hand was recently stung and swollen, and he records personal concerns such as hoping for God’s forgiveness after breaking a vow to drink no wine.

The Narrator’s social and professional encounters include receiving a money offer from Mr. Shaw, going with My Lord from the Wardrobe and Whitehall to Deptford, and finally departing by wherry as the yacht salutes him. He records visits to his Lady, talks with his father and Mr. Evans, news of Lord Sandwich, meetings with departing travelers, a death, a marriage proposal, and outings to dinner and the theatre. Throughout, he remains an unnamed first-person observer who documents arguments, financial dealings, travel, family interactions, and public and private affairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-047</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brampton">
      <data key="d0">Brampton</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Brampton is a village closely associated with Samuel Pepys and his family, serving as a significant recurring location in his diary. The place is linked to the Pepys family through property and residence: Samuel Pepys’s father traveled there to settle and later lived in the village, and it was also the home of Robert Pepys, Samuel’s uncle, who lived and died in Brampton. The diarist himself repeatedly stayed, dined, and slept in the village with his father, and on at least one occasion rode there to find his father well, though he later returned home with a sad heart.

Brampton was a focus of family matters and business. Samuel’s father corresponded with him from Brampton, and a box containing Samuel’s uncle’s papers arrived from the village. The family spent several days there putting affairs in order and inspecting the fields, especially after Uncle Robert’s death, when the uncle’s corpse was at Brampton and his burial took place there. Property discussions also centered on Brampton: it was a place where the diarist intended to purchase property, and specifically where Mr. Norbury’s wife owned a house and land that were discussed with the narrator as a possible acquisition.

Additionally, Brampton served as a base for daily activities; the narrator and his father stayed there after walks and returned for supper and bed. The father was often alone in Brampton, prompting the diarist to write letters to him, and the father also planned or made trips there to settle matters before Samuel’s mother would come. The village thus functioned both as a family home and as a hub for estate and inheritance affairs during the period recorded in Pepys’s diary.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445878</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The King">
      <data key="d0">The King</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The King, Charles II of England, is the monarch referenced throughout these diary entries. He was aboard his yacht at Greenwich when the narrator’s party arrived, and he was at Whitehall when My Lord took leave before beginning his journey; the Portuguese Ambassador also came to Whitehall to take leave of him. The King attended Sir William Davenant’s Opera, where the scene opened only after his arrival, and he was later present at the Theatre during the play “The Joviall Crew.” On another occasion, he attended the Opera with the Duke and Duchess of York, and the Duke planned to consult him about Navy money.

The King’s mistress at the time was Mrs. Palmer. Mr. Hill warned that the King would be forced to favour Presbytery, or the City would leave him. The King was expected to come and adjourn Parliament, and was later encountered on the water in his barge, on his way to adjourn the House. The previous day, he had participated in a stag hunt that tired out the horses of the other hunters. He also gave a billet to Clarendon and wrote a warrant naming Sir Stephen Fox. Finally, Mrs. Crispe intended to ask the King to be godfather to her daughter.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pall">
      <data key="d0">Pall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pall, an individual associated with the household of the diarist (Samuel Pepys), appears in several accounts as a young woman and dependent relative. She attended the christening of Kate Joyce's child and stayed overnight at Pepys's father's house because she was feeling unwell. In another episode, she was taken by water with Lady Jem through the bridge to view ships and the diarist's house. She also joined the diarist, his wife, and Aunt Wight for supper at Aunt Wight's house.

Within the household, Pall was described as having grown proud and idle, leading the diarist to resolve not to keep her in his employment or care. He informed his own father that Pall should not remain with him any longer. At another point, he angrily declared he would keep her no more, while her father likewise refused to take responsibility for her. Because she was regarded as troublesome, she was eventually sent into the country with the diarist's mother and father. Prior to her departure, she was left to perform all the household work until another maid could be hired; thereafter, she was destined to go into the countryside with the diarist's mother. These various depictions together portray Pall as a source of tension within the household, leading to her removal from the diarist's immediate domestic sphere.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="My Lady">
      <data key="d0">My Lady</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">“My Lady” is the woman referred to by that title in Samuel Pepys’s diary; she is a noblewoman associated with My Lord and was hostess at The Wardrobe. Pepys and his wife dined with My Lady and the children at the Wardrobe, where she showed them great hospitality and favour. The diarist visited her at her house on the 22nd, dined with her on several occasions—including at noon on April 28 and after meeting Uncle Wight—and accompanied Dean Fuller to a gathering at The Wardrobe where My Lady and the whole company were merry and well. In her husband’s absence, she returned to modest, poor housekeeping but continued to entertain.

Her close connections to the Pepys circle are reflected in the accounts. She was the mother of three sons who were sent to the narrator’s house. She mourned the death of her brother, Mr. Samuel Crew. Pepys supped with her while consulting about Lord Hinchingbroke’s illness, and he told her of My Lord’s sickness and recovery. He also informed her of Robert Pepys’s death. At another dinner at The Wardrobe, she called the diarist up to talk and innocently told him about Mrs. Crispe’s plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788445898</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Strand">
      <data key="d0">The Strand</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cf47789c7f6b678113755e09e7199138-chunk-015</data>
