    <edge source="Narrator" target="Narrator's Wife">
      <data key="d7">10.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relation (Narrator, Narrator’s Wife) describes the married couple in the diary entries, identifying Narrator’s Wife as the wife of the Narrator and the Narrator as her husband; he consistently refers to her as his wife throughout the diary. Their shared activities include walking together on the leads and then going to bed, dining at home and attending church together on the Lord’s day, and going together to The Theatre and later to the Narrator’s Father’s house.

In everyday matters, the Narrator gave Narrator’s Wife L4 to spend on lace and other things. She also relayed news to him, telling him that Sir W. Pen’s daughter was a plain girl rather than the expected great beauty, and informing him about Balty’s condition and asking the Narrator to do something for Balty.

A note on her health states that she was not sick but out of order, and a faced white coat was made from one of her petticoats.</data>
      <data key="d9">asks help for Balty,attended church,dined with,gave money,husband of,married to,married_to,reported_to,spouse_of,told,walked with,went to bed,wife of</data>
